Z||000024||kslish||04-11-2001||08:12 AM||||Okay, I have both PCs and Macs (I'm also a part-time Apple employee).  I was going to stay out of this thread but there is so much incorrect information in it I had to put my 2 cents in.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierogt88:<BR><B>You were not incorrect.  Bill Gates has controlling interest in Apple.  I am not sure whether it is through microsoft or whether it is Bill himself, but Bill is the top dog there now.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, Apple is NOT owned by Microsoft, Bill Gates or anyone else.  Steve Jobs is the current CEO of Apple Computer.<P>How this incorrect information started is because back in 1996-1997 Apple was in financial ruins since former CEO Michael Spindler had ran the company into the ground.  At the time, Apple was in the middle of suing Microsoft over Microsoft illegally stealing code from Apple's Quicktime technology and using it in Windows Media Player.  This was basically proven in court and Microsoft would have lost and been found guilty of patent infringement.  To save face, and since Apple needed the money at the time, they settled out of court with Microsoft paying Apple a HUGE cash settlement.  It was sort of a win for both companies, Microsoft got a license to use Apple's technology legally, and Apple got themselves out of a hole.<P>Around this same time Apple turned around and purchased NeXT computer, which brought back the return of Steve Jobs (NeXT was the computer company he founded when he left Apple in the mid-80's), and after winning support from Apple's Board of Directors, Steve Jobs regained his old seat as Apple's CEO.  Under his direction, Apple has returned to profitability, it has revamped its entire product line, it has a new UNIX based OS (Mac OS X), and has 5.4 billion (yes, billion) in cash reserves to use in case the company ever stumbles again.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>I program industrial software and talk to Microsoft reps all the time.  The advantage that the Mac's had in the graphics department was from specializatoin, not because it was a better machine.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And you actually trust Microsoft reps?  They'll probably tell you that MS-DOS was created by Microsoft itself also.  Microsoft hasn't created one piece of new technology that they haven't stolen or "aquired" from the original inventor/innovator.  They are the best marketing company in the world though.<P>(for any computer history buffs out there, MS-DOS was actually written by Tim Paterson who worked at Seattle Computer.  He sold what is now known as MS-DOS to Bill Gates and  Microsoft around 1980 for $30,000.  And the rest is history.  I bet that Peterson guy has never forgiven himself for that one, but $30,000 probably seemed like a lot of money at the time).  Another example is how Internet Explorer is really just an enhanced version of NCSA Mosaic for those of us that remember the Mosaic web browser.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>The reason everyone seems to like the Mac better for graphics apps is because Apple has always wined and dined the graphics software companies, so they developed the software for that platform.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And there is something wrong with that?  It's called getting the business.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>And without the corner on graphics there isn't much left to justify a Mac.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>C'mon, don't be so closed minded.<P>Even though it seems like I'm coming across as a hard-core Apple psycho.  I'm not.  Windows and Macs peacefully coexist in my house.  I just advise people to use the best tool for the job.  For some things it's a Windows machine, for some things it's a Mac, and there is some other things that are just done better on a UNIX/Linux box (a cheap stable web server for example).<P>DISenchanted, do you have a new Titanium PowerBook?  Those are slick....<P>Ken S.<P><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||DISenchanted||04-11-2001||08:24 AM||||I do.  And I love it...  I can't believe the looks it gets.<P>Especially running 9.1, X, and Win98...<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000026||fierogt88||04-11-2001||11:13 AM||||Oops.  I stand corrected on the ownership issue.  Guess that's what I get for listening to those Microsoft guys...  This is not the first time I've had egg on my face from that hehe.  I guess I thought they knew what they were talking about, and it jived with my general knowledge of lots of money changing hands and most of the Apple employees being kinda ticked off.<P>And yes, kslish, wining and dining is called getting the business, but in such a vertical market as graphics, you also have to have a real technological edge.  Right now, and for a long time now, both Alphas and SGI's make the Mac's run for cover in this market.  Note that I didn't say PC's.  What other markets does Apple target?  I can't think of any.  And therein lies the weakness of the platform.  <P>And to say that Microsoft hasn't created one piece of new technology is just nuts.  I program industrial automation technology and I can tell you that there is no other platform that even comes close to the advances they have made in automation, robotics and machine control.  This market has been so profitable to them they started a little project known as Windows 2000 that was built around technology that THEY invented.  Win2k was not built for "business" it was built for industrial apps.  90% of the new technology in there you will never see unless you have to deal with it in an automation/control system.  So where is all the new tech from Apple?<P>I would like to clarify that I am not anti-apple.  They just seem to consistently target the graphics market, which they have no chance of cornering.  They're good machines but for the same money I can get an Alpha that runs all the same software at twice the speed, or i can buy a powerhouse PC that has 100 times the versatility and available software titles.  And then there is that god-awful one-button mouse....  which has to be my biggest complaint about them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  course it's been three years since i actually sat down at one so they probably fixed that.... oh wait they wouldn't do that because someone might acuse them of stealing the idea or something....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||9
Z||000027||kslish||04-11-2001||04:30 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierogt88:<BR><B>What other markets does Apple target?  I can't think of any.  And therein lies the weakness of the platform.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let's see, you've got the video editing market (Apple Final Cut Pro is becoming THE standard in video editing apps and iDVD is the first OEM package that permits you to burn your own DVD videos that play on commercial DVD players), the primary and higher education markets, the scientific market, the digital audio market (visit a recording studio and 9 times out of 10 they'll be using Macs).  They also target the new user trying to get onto the Internet (Why try to convert a died-in-the-wool Windows user when there is still a ton of people without a PC).  Soon they will be moving into the server market since Mac OS X has BSD Unix at its core.  There is probably much more, my brain is just dead at the moment.<P>One of the coolest uses for Macs I've seen recently was at CNN.  CNN has outfitted all of their remote reporters with an Apple Powerbook and a professional digital camcorder.  A reporter can shoot their own clip, download it from the camcorder onto the Powerbook through Firewire, edit the video in Final Cut Pro, and prepare it for broadcast.  No more need for a huge camera and editing equipment back in the truck.  Big cost and time savings for CNN.<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>So where is all the new tech from Apple?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh yeah, as for Firewire (Sony calls it iLink), that was invented by Apple and they get royalties from other companies to use it.  So there is an example of a just one of a number of technologies invented by Apple.<P>In the forseeable future, there will always be Microsoft and Windows (after all it is the "standard"), but like it or not, Apple isn't going anywhere.  Steve Jobs and Apple are one of the computer industry's main visionaries to lead the way, and Bill Gates and the Microsoft empire will always have the money and resources to compete.  I like Apple being the scrappy underdog and wouldn't want to change it.  <P>Oh yeah, the OEM mice on Macs still only have one button....but they are optical mice now    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken S.<P><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||Standard||04-11-2001||09:19 PM||||This is almost as bad as the religion threads  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm a PC guy.  I grew up on PC's... started on the good ole' 8086.  I remember when we got a 386 dx/20, man did that thing blaze  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I like being able to choose how I build my system, and being able to build it cheap.  Everytime I use a Mac I'm left wondering how they manage to keep selling them... and that damn one-button mouse!  We have iMacs at work and those things are downright irritating, and slow... but this is just my opinion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As for mac's having the education market cornered, that's because they practically give them away.  And they are easy to use... but I think it's kind of sad when people choose a computer because they can get it in a certain color and it looks "cute"... just a sign of the overall lack of intelligent thought that's pervasive in our society.. <P>[/rant]<P>not directed towards anybody on the forum.. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||Fiero5||04-11-2001||10:17 PM||||First off, my family and I all own Macs. We have owned both, but we prefer the more powerfull and versatile Macintosh.<BR>Just for giggles, did you know that a G4 is 4 times faster than an Intel Pentium 4?<BR>Did you know that most games are created on Macs, even the ones made by Microsoft, then the PC version is ported over to Windows?<P>Let me say before I get people yelling that I am just a Mac guy and that I don't know PC's, that I am both Microsoft certified and Apple certified. I am currently one of only like 20 IBM authorized technicians in NH and VT. I do alot of side work on Thinkpads.<P>I have been working on both platforms since 1986, going thru computer hardware and software training all these years at different companies along the way. I have done many a network design since and then actually done the whole layout and setup from start to finish with both Novell and NT. I am currently the technology coordinator and repair technician at a very large high school- Hanover High (right next door to Dartmouth College) and maintain the networking for both the Mac and PC side. I also help maintain the computers in general thru out the school. <P>When I was in retail (electronics manager for the 10 million dollar a year dept.) at Staples, no Macs there since like 96 I think, people would discover how knowledgable I was on computers and would ask..."So, if you were to buy one, which one would you get?"  I always loved it when they asked me that! Why a Mac? Because when I come home at night, I want to "use" the machine, not spend the last few hours of the day making it work.<P>You can argue all day long about who invented/stole the GUI and why Apple has a smaller share than PC's, yadda, yadda, yadda.<BR>Yeah, Xerox had a GUI on display before Apple did. Who cares now. Microsoft got away with alot worse.<P>The fact remains that Macs have been "THE" industry leader since day one and continue to be today. So what if Xerox technically had the first GUI, which the Woz man took that idea and his friend Jobs helped him run with it. So what if Gates is a marketing genius  selling crap as an OS. Yeah it runs, but it could be alot better. Macs had the first running consumer based full GUI. A few years later after Bill Gates had done some piece work at Apple when he used to contract out, he miraculously comes up with Windows. He tries to sell it to IBM who turns him down. Dumb move there! Bill then starts Microsoft and markets it himself.<BR> <BR>Mac's had the mouse first, then PC's years later. Let's move ahead a few years. Apple released a new technology called the track pad on the Powerbook 520 series, now years later all but two PC laptops (IBM and Toshiba) are using the trackpad technology. As Intel struggled to keep their aging technology from overheating on each new chip release, Apple dropped the 680X0 chip technology and decided it was time for a better faster technology. Apple worked in a joint venture with IBM and Motorolla to come up with the new up to 4 times faster PowerPC technology.<P>Blah, blah I know.  Just thought I would help set the record straight.<P>I know some would like to argue that Macs are week in software. Hmm, I have yet not been able to get any program I wanted to run on a Mac. Well, there was this $9.99 Walmart special racing game  LOL  <BR>For those, I can run Virtual PC  LOL<P>Now I know a bunch of PC lovin' Mac trashin' yahoos are gonna fire back with a bunch of silly this and that of why they think a PC rules over a Mac. Make sure when you attempt this, try backing it up with facts.<P>One person said that it is what you are accustomed too. He also said he couldn't figure Macs out. I don't understand when people say these statements. Windows is a worked over semi copy of the Mac OS. Since Windows looks and runs very similar, how can you not easily figure one out from the other. That's like saying you can't figure out a 4 speed since using a 5 speed. I guess people say what they want to attempt to justify their view point. Granted the starting up and shutting down is different, and to eject the disk on Mac you have to trash it. Can't be any worse than selecting start to shut down a PC  LOL<P>Now I don't want any flaming here, as I know everyone else is gonna want a turn now after this one  LOL<P>Just try to remember as you post back your responses the difference between what you like for whatever reason and what is actually better.  :o)<P>Steve||||reg||1
Z||000030||kslish||04-12-2001||07:30 AM||||Fiero5, all good points but before someone jumps all over you about a few small inaccuracies in some of your statements, I thought I'd help patch them up for you.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR><B>Just for giggles, did you know that a G4 is 4 times faster than an Intel Pentium 4?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is true but only when comparing chips of equal Mhz.  Unfortunately, due to some problems Motorola has had with getting their chips to yield properly, the fastest consumer G4 available is around 750 Mhz.  This should change sometime later this year and the 1 Ghz barrier should be broken on the G4.  A 700 Mhz G4 though stacks up nicely to a 1.4 Ghz P4 though.  It amazes me how people I consult don't realize that clock speed (Mhz) isn't everything, I constantly get questions like "how does your Mac run Photoshop filters quicker than my PC when my PC has a 'faster' processor?"<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>A few years later after Bill Gates had done some piece work at Apple when he used to contract out, he miraculously comes up with Windows. He tries to sell it to IBM who turns him down. Dumb move there! Bill then starts Microsoft and markets it himself.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Microsoft has been around since the late 70's...Bill G. started Microsoft way before he started the contract work for Apple.  The real truth is that he got Apple to give Microsoft a prototype Mac way before they were intoduced to the public with the promise that Microsoft would have some software productivity apps available for sale when the Mac first went on sale in 1984 (and they did keep that promise).  However, they also used their sneak peak at the Mac to as a basis to begin work on Windows 1.0.  I think Steve Jobs is still pissed at himself for letting Gates pull that one over on him. <BR> <BR>A good movie about the beginnings of Apple and Microsoft (although they exaggerated some details) is The Pirates of Silicon Valley produced by TNT.  It has Noah Wyle (from ER) as Steve Jobs and Anthony Michael Hall as Bill Gates and they both nailed their characters pretty well.<P>You can check out the TNT site about the movie here: <BR> <A HREF="http://tnt.turner.com/movies/tntoriginals/pirates/" TARGET=_blank>http://tnt.turner.com/movies/tntoriginals/pirates/</A> <P>and the preview trailer here:<BR> <A HREF="http://tnt.turner.com/movies/tntoriginals/pirates/html/trailer.html" TARGET=_blank>http://tnt.turner.com/movies/tntoriginals/pirates/html/trailer.html</A> <P>Ken S.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 04-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Fiero5||04-12-2001||09:15 AM||||A few years later after Bill Gates had done some piece work at Apple when he used to contract out, he miraculously comes up with Windows. He tries to sell it to IBM who turns him down. Dumb move there! Bill then starts Microsoft and markets it himself.<P>Microsoft has been around since the late 70's...Bill G. started Microsoft way before he started the contract work for Apple.<P><BR>All true.  I was attempting to be as brief as possible given the subject matter. Microsoft was the name he came up with for business reasons (taxes and all) when he deciced to go and sub contract out, but he really didn't gear up the company until he decided to market Windows. Microsoft didn't start to become a houshold name until Windows started showing up as the prefered OS for the PC's.<P>Give Bill credit, as he can market almost anything. I have always said that he could probably convince the majority of the PC buying public that a new pencil was a new fangled computer device if he wanted to. LOL||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mach10||04-12-2001||09:53 PM||||First off, I'm pro-PC, mostly 'cos I do games. PCs RULE for games. I did the CompSci thing a while ago, but lost interest.<BR>Now:<BR>FOUR WORDS: Memory Management. User Interface.<BR>Those are the little brass nails in the Mac's  coffin.<P>Memory Management: When in the HELL do you EVER want to have to "defrag" (so to speak) memory to run another application?!? YE-GODS!<P>User Interface: "Oh, I hit EXIT, but I don't REALLY want to kill it. No, I want it to run invisibly in my background, not freeing up resources." Horse crap. But then, that was Apple's bone-headed marketing team, not the architecture not living up to it's promise. The Mac hardware is very efficient. Unfortunately crippled by an aging user-interface. I'd switch to Linux sooooo fast....<P>Those are what did it for me... As for Mac cornering the graphics department? Can you say "Sparq Station?" Silicon Graphics will blow the cheap blue plastic off of anything Mac has to offer, albeight at 3 times the price  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>MS did with Windows what Mac failed. What the hell are you going to do with only one mouse button?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Just kidding. Both machines DO have their strong points. Macs are GREAT small office performers, and their DVD support blows the pants off of anything I've experienced on PC as yet. As for gaming? I haven't seen enough of a move in that direction to make me even consider a change. <P>As for the speed thingy, you are comparing Apples to Oranges. Macintosh runs a RISC based architecture. PCs run CISC. COMPLETELY different ways to do the same thing, i.e., put stuff on the screen.<BR>4x faster? Right. Pull the other one. It has bells on. A Dual G4-500Mhz Mac is able to keep up with a 1Ghz Athlon. Barely. Even the 1.4Ghz P4 is able to top it in almost every test, and is(was) $700 cheaper.See the link here:  <A HREF="http://www.barefeats.com/pentium.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.barefeats.com/pentium.html</A> <P>Sorry. While Macs do have some cool features, they just aren't my thing. Nice thing, though, is that Apple is *FINALLY* waking up to the fact that some people don't just use computers for work, and are starting to implement some cool things. Personally, I'll stay with the PC until Mac proves itself as versatile and cheap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000033||DISenchanted||04-13-2001||06:10 AM||||I think you read that site wrong.  I only saw the mac lose once.  Plus it looks like they were using an older mac.  Not that is should be mind-blowing in difference.  The bus speed on the one they tested was only 100mhz.  All of the new ones have 133mhz.  Still I wouldn't want everyone to own a mac.  Same reasons I wouldn't want everyone to own a FIERO...<P>Mach10:  You might be interested in Macintosh OSX...<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000034||kslish||04-13-2001||08:30 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Apple's bone-headed marketing team<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, I'm part of that marketing team.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'll be the first to admit that the Mac is weak in games...but that is what I have a Playstation for.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>The game thing may change soon though, as  Nvidia's GeForce3 graphics card is going to be available on the Mac months before it is available on the PC.<P>I'll also admit that Mac OS 9.1 and earlier was less than perfect.  Some of the code at the core of it was written in 1984, and it has been patched and hacked at so many times to get it to run on newer faster machines I'm amazed that Apple's engineers were able to make it as stable as it is (not having protected memory is a pain in the rear though).  Plus Mac OS 9.1 and earlier can't take advantage of more than one processor at a time.<P>However all of this is changing.  With the introduction of Mac OS X, I've enjoyed spending a lot of time with Unix and Linux techies that are absolutely going nuts over Mac OS X.  It fixes everything that was wrong with the Mac OS (OS X has open source Unix at it's core).  Due to its easy to use GUI, it will be the first implementation to bring Unix to the mainstream everyday desktop consumer...something Linux has failed to do with the KDE and Gnome GUIs.<P>Ken S.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 04-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||Fiero5||04-13-2001||11:26 AM||||That's funny. Usually I hear statements that Macs are not good business machines (which by the way is far from the truth). Here, I am hearing people say the opposite. Makes me laugh  :o)<P>I always love when people bash the Mac saying things like it isn't good for games, or that it is weak in some area. <BR>Memory management problems? What's with that baseless gripe? If your running 20MB of ram maybe. Get over 64MB or even into 128MB range where you should be these days and the Mac does it stuff at a level that PC's can only dream about. But the memory management is what helps when you are running multiple apps at the same time. Oh yeah, I keep forgetting that PC users aren't used to that high end capability yet. I know that somewhere on the web there is a chart that shows how far behind Windows is compared to where the Mac OS is.<P>My favorite though has to be when people say things like they will waite till the Mac proves itself or get's the glitches out.<BR>Everytime a PC owner uses Windows, they are proving how well the Mac OS is. The Mac proved itself by having 80% of it's technology copied on the PC side. Reference to my earlier responce. <P>As for glitches, I remember when MS was releasing multiple fixes/patches almost daily when Win 95 came out. Win 98 had less, but that was because of the fact that Win 98 was 95 with all the fixes allready added. The Apple OS has had a fraction of the patches released that MS Win has to date. Give me a break.<P>Try reinstalling just the Windows OS on a PC without effecting any other programs or files.<P>I was typing a response earlier on a PC (5 month old Dell Optiplex) and I got a lovely Windows illegal operation error message and had to restart the machine. Now I am on an I-Mac running 9.0.4.<P>As far as a Mac getting beaten by a PC in tests with comparable processors, it hasn't happened*. Maybe in one bar out of twenty the PC handled something well, but that's it. The Mac rules hands down. Graphics, mathmatical equations, memory allocation, code handling like with C++ and on and on. *Sure it's easy to make the Mac look slow when you pit a newer Pentium PC against an older PowerPC Mac. Duh!<P>I do like the PC's for two reasons though. It makes me money keeping them running (10 PC's break down on average to one Mac and the PC's take 5 times longer to fix) and it gives all of us something to argue about.  LOL<P>Flame suit on and ready for battle  :o)||||reg||1
Z||000036||DISenchanted||04-13-2001||11:32 PM||||The update for OSX build 4L13 is out...  Woo Hoo!!!<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000037||Mach10||04-14-2001||04:12 AM||||Dis: Yes, you are right. That was a *BAD* example. I have seen benchmarks to the effect I listed, and I made the mistake of assuming that they'd all be the same, so I posted the first graphs I saw, without really reading. If I find them, I'll post, but until then, consider me corrected  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Fiero5: That's besides the point. "add more ram to correct problems" is a STUPID way out of the problem. I stand by my statement. MEMORY MANAGEMENT FOR THE MAC SUCKS DONKEY BALLS. Adding more hardware to correct a inherent flaw in the OS is beyond stupid. If the Mac's so awesome, why CAN'T it do all that with just 20Mb? I'm running all my photo-editing, dtp, CAD, and vector graphic tools on my 266Mhz P-II w/ 128Mb. Works fine, and fast as I need it. Oh, and it cost about 1/3 of what the mac I'd need for this would cost. The system I'm running right now for pleasure is a 466Celeron w. 192. As for running multiple apps? Oh, gee, I'm not, like, doing THAT RIGHT NOW or anything... Ooh, let's see. IE50 in foreground, Winamp playing tunes, Virus scanning, ICQ, Excel, Word, MSN Messenger, firewall, and a telnet session running at the same time. What's your definition of multiple threads? I'll grab a screen shot of any combination that you'd like.||||reg||1
Z||000038||DISenchanted||04-14-2001||04:02 PM||||Mach10:  You should check out Mac OSX.  It is Unix with a really pretty interface.  Plus, apple just released "Darwin" it's kernel for x86 based machines...<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000039||Mach10||04-14-2001||04:29 PM||||I've seen some screenshots... Pretty sharp. IF MacOS is releasing an OS for the x86 platform, and proves to be useful, then I'd seriously consider it. But again, I'm a gamer, and Macs aren't that great for games (yet). The Radeon chipset is cool, though, even IF it's ATI  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ok, after slamming macs in this post several times, in all fairness, I should point out some good things:<BR>1) Fast mouse refresh, without any special hardware/REG-hacks<BR>2) Built-in networking<BR>3) Really cool shortcut tracking<BR>4) The "cube" is the most bad-ass'd toaster I've EVER seen =D<BR>5) OS runs pretty much at the same speed regardless of processor/memory etc. (fast)<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000040||Fiero5||04-14-2001||06:27 PM||||"If the Mac's so awesome, why CAN'T it do all that with just 20Mb? I'm running all my photo-editing, dtp, CAD, and vector graphic tools on my 266Mhz P-II w/ 128Mb. Works fine, and fast as I need it."<P>Ha, Ha, Ha, Ha, Ha...that has to be the dumbest comment yet! Just re-read this brilliant comment! Mine runs on 128MB fine, so why can't the Mac do the same thing on 20MB?<BR>Ha, Ha, Ha, Ha, Ha<BR>This is a good example of what Mac people have to deal with constantly.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000041||DISenchanted||04-15-2001||02:14 AM||||I've been thinking about mounting a "cube" in  my Fiero...  I think Nissan beat me to the idea though.<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000042||Mach10||04-15-2001||11:37 PM||||I knew I had forgotten a smilie somewhere :P That *was* supposed to be sarcastic <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>It's actually the other way around... WE have to deal with Mac users. Try ISP tech support for a while, and troubleshooting older mac systems on DHCP. Yeah. Open Transport... *shudder*<P>It's gotten a lot better in the last while though. MAC-9 is pretty decent, and Mac-X looks really cool, but I am STILL staying with by beloved PC. What's the fun of never rebooting? :P &lt;--- There! I did it!<P>||||reg||10
Z||000043||Fiero5||04-16-2001||09:31 PM||||:P<P>Well OK, I guess all is forgiven then  :o)<P>Steve||||reg||1
Z||000044||beer_stud_21||04-17-2001||05:30 PM||||Well, being a long time mac head I cant stay away from this thread.  After trying to fight the good fight for close to 6 years I'm tired of trying to force PC users to see why they should switch to macs.  I've also spent a year doing tech support for Apple and troubleshooting just about every PPC desktop mac ever made.  (Even troubleshot a TAM once- 20th Anniversary Mac)  After that expierence I can tell everyone that both systems can have their problems.   I just spent 15 minutes writing this reply and then deleted half of it because I was rambling on and on.  Basically I want to say this:  The mac has been given a really bad rap over the years.  Some of it has been justified but a lot of it has not.  No matter what anybody says if the mac wasnt around for the last 17 yrs the computer industry would be in a sad state of affairs.  Apple is one of the only computer companies in the word with enough balls to innovate.   They brought personal computers the mouse, the 3 1/4 floppy, the CD rom drive, ethernet, GUI (graphical user interface), Desktop Publishing,  and most recently wireless networking, and Digital Video.  From the beginning Apple has been really shitty at marketing what they have.  If they would have hired Bill Gates has their marketing director in the early 80's 99% of the world would be using an apple computer right now.  In about 6 months Apple is going to be opening their own chain of retail stores across the country.  Very much like the gateway country stores.  I will bet money anyone who claims to dislike macs for whatever reason would spend 20 minutes in one of these stores will leave with a far better impression of Macs than they came in with.  There is just something about surfing the internet from a Powerbook G4 titanium completely wirelessly or buring your own DVD movie you just made that will make you say "This is F*cking Cool!"  In that way macs are a lot like fieros i guess.<P>OK, im done.<BR>Btw:  Any mac guys on here that want to BS or need help with something my icq number is 8273989 feel free to add me to your icq.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000045||Steve Normington||04-19-2001||04:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero5:<BR>Why a Mac? Because when I come home at night, I want to "use" the machine, not spend the last few hours of the day making it work.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, of course, that's why you like Fieros, because you never have to spend the last few hours of the day making it work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||12
Z||000046||Mach10||04-21-2001||06:36 PM||||Beer_stud: Saying that MAC is better 'cos they were there first is like saying that Ford rules because of the Model A.<P>I won't argue that the MAC-Os is not a stable platform. It is. It has some STUPID implementations, as well as a somewhat retarded user interface, but it is fast, and it is reliable. It ALSO has some really neat features that M$ would do well to implement. The major argument between PC vs Mac is the software out for it. The Mac just doesn't have as much out for it. It *IS* getting better, and with all this really coolnew hardware for the Mac coming out, it looks as though Mac may actually get in on the mainstream. Until then, I stay with my beloved PC.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000047||MontanaFiero||04-22-2001||04:57 AM||||OK since no one else has anything to say about the "ONE button mouse" then I guess I will have to come up with the rebuttal... Sure, apple made a mistake by only having a one button mouse, but by doing that they cater to the simple minded, which in the computer world is important. The mac DOES support multiple button mice, and my three button scrolling track-ball does everything your two and three button pc mouse does. All for an extra whopping twenty bucks.<P>By the way, I spend more time trying to keep my (and my parents') <I>wonderful</I> PC from crashing and WAITING FOR IT TO BOOT UP that I am just so happy to have the use of my virtually crash free iMac DV . And my iMac can boot up twice in the time it takes Windows to load.<P>I also like the benefit of a computer (the Macintosh) that can be a macintosh and a PC at the same time by  simply running Virtual PC. Now I have the best of both worlds.||||reg||1
Z||000048||DISenchanted||04-23-2001||05:09 AM||||I have some complaints about Macs.<P>First: They are more expensive then their P.C. counterparts.  It is a cold hard fact.  The only way I can justify it is that they are a superior quality.  Argue all you want, but put my Titanium Laptop next to ANY P.C. laptop right now and I will put money that 8 out of 10 people would choose the TiBook.<P>The user experience is also a lot more elegant.  It's like like comparing a Fiero to a NSX.  Both are wonderful cars.  You could spend a lot of money on your Fiero to make it perform like a NSX and the overall cost would still be lower.  Granted they both get the job done, but which one do you think provides a better "experience"?<P>I love my Fiero's. Even the two that blew up, but they are not a NSX and they never will be.<P>Second: There is less software for a Mac then a P.C.  Main reason, there are more P.C.'s in the world.<P>I have learned to deal with this because I know any important software can be made to run on a Mac.  If there is a program that you have to have, that is not made for a Mac you can run an emulator.<P>Third: They have not done a good job of marketing.  They have sat silent during the whole megahertz race, not bothering to mention that it has nothing to do with performance.<P>I know that my Mac is faster then a P.C. at double the mhz, but do you?  Does your mother?  At least Intel screwed up enough to start letting the rest of the world know(P4-Athalon).<P>I feel that they are better machines.  They are more expensive though and that would be the only reason I would not purchase one.<P>My goal is not to change anyone's mind.  It's to inform them so that they could make a better decision.<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
